  {
   "title": "Small corpus",
   "text": "4.0 million words, versus hundreds of millions for the main languages. Frequency values are unreliable, so there are no frequency bands, only the all-words generators. The reference lexicon has 64,990 words, so generators, neighbourhood measures and cluster filters are rougher than for the main languages."
  },
  {
   "title": "Newspaper vocabulary added",
   "text": "47,047 of the reference words come from a newspaper corpus (Leipzig Corpora Collection, mal_newscrawl_2011_100K, 1.3 million words); 17,943 come from the subtitle list. Written sources are more formal than subtitles and contain more names and foreign words. Frequencies of the corpus-only words come from that corpus and are not comparable with subtitle frequencies."
  },
  {
   "title": "Abugida: CV and length",
   "text": "Malayalam is an abugida: CV (orthographic) counts every consonant letter as C plus an inherent vowel V unless a vowel sign or virama follows; vowel signs and vowel letters are V; chillu letters and anusvara are C. Old-style chillu spellings (consonant + virama + joiner) were converted to the atomic chillu letters. Length is counted in aksharas."
  }
 ]
